INT. OBSERVATORY DOME - NIGHT

A great brass telescope points at a slit of stars. A circular stair winds to a gallery. On the floor, chalk outlines a body that has been moved.

INSPECTOR MORTIMER GALE, fifties, a heavy coat, a notebook, circles the chalk slowly. DR. AURELIO BANERJEE, forties, the observatory's director, in shirtsleeves, stands by the telescope with his hands clasped.

GALE
The night assistant, Mr. Pell. Found here at six.

BANERJEE
I arrived at six-fifteen.

GALE
Did he work alone?

BANERJEE
Mostly. He liked the quiet.

GALE
He also liked your office. The lock was forced.

Banerjee doesn't flinch.

BANERJEE
He had a habit of borrowing my star charts.

GALE
(writing)
Borrowing. He took them home?

BANERJEE
He took them to his sister's.

GALE
In another city.

BANERJEE
He was a collector.

Gale stops circling. He taps the notebook.

GALE
Dr. Banerjee, a man doesn't lock himself in a dome with a telescope and fall down a staircase.

BANERJEE
He was clumsy.

GALE
He was a retired gymnast.

A beat. Banerjee turns to the telescope. Adjusts a brass dial that doesn't need adjusting.

BANERJEE
Then he was careless.

GALE
Or pushed.

BANERJEE
By whom? There is no one here but me.

The dome door at the bottom of the stair swings open. Footsteps. A flashlight sweeps the floor.

CONSTABLE PIKE, twenties, rain on his helmet, hurries up. He holds a sealed envelope.

PIKE
Inspector. This came from the lab.

Gale takes it. Opens it. Reads. Banerjee watches him read.

GALE
Fingerprints on the brass rail. Two sets.

BANERJEE
Naturally. Mine, and Pell's.

GALE
One is Pell's.

BANERJEE
And the other?

Gale looks up, his face unreadable.

GALE
It's not yours.

Banerjee blinks. He grips the telescope.

BANERJEE
That's impossible.

PIKE
Inspector, there's more. The second set matches a man missing from Lisbon.

GALE
(to Banerjee)
Who else has a key to this dome?

BANERJEE
Only the board. And Pell.

GALE
Then he let someone in.

BANERJEE
Why would he do that at midnight?

GALE
(sharp)
That's what I'd like to know.

Banerjee crosses to the stair. Pike steps aside. Banerjee slows at the first step, looks at the chalk, looks back at Gale.

BANERJEE
He was afraid of someone.

GALE
You knew?

BANERJEE
Last week, he asked me for a new lock.

GALE
And you refused.

BANERJEE
The budget--

GALE
(exploding)
The budget! A man is dead, Doctor! A man you let sit alone in the dark with a stranger's key!

The telescope creaks. Pike backs a step. Banerjee, white, grips the rail.

BANERJEE
I did not know.

GALE
Next time, Doctor, ask.

He closes the notebook with a snap. Pike holds the door. Gale walks out, his boots echoing on the iron stair.
